<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zakat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zakat is the obligatory almsgiving of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, one of the Five Pillars, levied as a proportion of certain kinds of wealth and distributed to the poor and other specified recipients. In Islamic states it also functioned as a tax. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> profile notes that each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> collected zakat on agricultural produce and livestock, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a>, remitting a portion to Sokoto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zakat.jpg" alt="Zakat" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SPQR10 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zakat_categories.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
